new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3a54155
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+## Deploying Fizzy to your own server
+
+Fizzy comes with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) config for running it on one Linux server. A single container runs the web server and the background jobs, keeps its SQLite databases and uploaded files on a Docker volume, and gets HTTPS from kamal-proxy with a Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server you can SSH into as root, with ports 80 and 443 open. Kamal installs Docker on it if it isn't there yet.
+- A hostname whose DNS points at that server, like `fizzy.example.com`.
+- An SMTP server or email provider. Fizzy signs people in with emailed codes, so email has to work.
+- Docker on your own machine, where the image gets built, and this repository with its gems installed (`bundle install`).
+
+### 1. Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+- `x-host`: your hostname. It's used as the server to SSH into, the host to get a certificate for, and the host in links Fizzy emails out.
+- `SMTP_ADDRESS`: your mail server. Uncomment `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_TLS` and friends if the defaults (port 587 with STARTTLS) don't fit.
+- `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`: the sender of every email. Use an address your mail server is allowed to send from.
+- `builder/arch`: change it to `arm64` if your server is ARM.
+
+### 2. Provide your secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` reads each secret from your environment. It's committed to git, so never write a credential into the file itself. Point it at your password manager instead if you prefer: the file shows how.
+
+| Secret | |
+| --- | --- |
+| `SECRET_KEY_BASE` | Required. Generate it with `bin/rails secret`. |
+| `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | Your mail server's credentials. Leave them unset if it doesn't need authentication. |
+| `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` | Optional, for browser push notifications. `.kamal/secrets` shows how to generate them. |
+
+Generate them once and reuse them on every deploy. Changing `SECRET_KEY_BASE` signs everyone out and breaks the images and files already attached to cards and comments.
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=... SMTP_USERNAME=... SMTP_PASSWORD=...
+bin/kamal secrets print # check what Kamal will hand to the container
+```
+
+### 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+The first deploy installs Docker on the server if needed, starts kamal-proxy, builds the image on your machine, pushes it to the server over SSH (no container registry account needed), and boots Fizzy. The proxy then fetches a certificate for your hostname.
+
+Then go to `https://your-host` and sign up. Fizzy hosts a single account that belongs to whoever signs up first, so do this right after the first deploy. Set `MULTI_TENANT: true` in `config/deploy.yml` if you want anyone to be able to sign up and create their own account.
+
+To ship changes later, run `bin/kamal deploy`. Kamal builds from your last git commit, so commit code changes before deploying them.
+
+### Your data
+
+The SQLite databases and uploaded files live in `/rails/storage`, on the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume. Each deploy replaces the container but keeps the volume, so your data carries over.
+
+That volume is the only copy of your data, so back it up. On the server it's under `/var/lib/docker/volumes/fizzy_storage/_data`. Copying a database file while Fizzy is writing to it can give you a broken copy. Use SQLite's `.backup` command instead (the `sqlite3` CLI is in the image), or copy the files while the app is stopped.
+
+### Running it
+
+- `bin/kamal logs` follows the logs.
+- `bin/kamal console` opens a Rails console. `bin/kamal shell` opens a shell, and `bin/kamal dbc` opens a SQLite console on the main database.
+- Background jobs run inside the web container, so there's no separate job server to manage.
+- On a server short on memory, lower `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY`. Both default to one process per CPU core.
+- Big account imports may need the proxy's 1GB upload limit and 30-second timeout raised: see `proxy` in `config/deploy.yml`.
+
+### Troubleshooting
+
+- **No sign-in email.** Check `bin/kamal logs` for SMTP errors, and make sure `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` is an address your mail server accepts.
+- **No certificate.** The hostname has to resolve to your server and ports 80 and 443 have to be reachable. If you're behind Cloudflare's proxy, set its SSL/TLS mode to "Full".
diff --git a/test/deploy_test.rb b/test/deploy_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bd992d0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class DeployTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  setup do
+    @config = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+    @web = @config.primary_role
+    @env = @web.env(@config.primary_host)
+  end
+
+  test "deploys a single container to a single server" do
+    assert_equal 1, @config.all_hosts.size
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], @config.roles.map(&:name)
+  end
+
+  test "runs background jobs inside the web container" do
+    assert_nil @web.cmd
+    assert_equal "true", @env.clear["SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA"].to_s
+  end
+
+  test "gets SSL from the proxy for the host the app links to" do
+    assert @web.ssl?
+    assert_equal [ @env.clear["APP_HOST"] ], @web.proxy.hosts
+  end
+
+  test "keeps storage on a volume that outlives each deploy" do
+    assert @config.volume_args.any? { |arg| arg.end_with?(":/rails/storage") }
+  end
+
+  test "sends email through the configured mail server" do
+    assert @env.clear["SMTP_ADDRESS"].present?
+    assert @env.clear["MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS"].present?
+  end
+
+  test "reads every secret from .kamal/secrets" do
+    assert_includes @env.secret_keys, "SECRET_KEY_BASE"
+
+    @env.secret_keys.each do |key|
+      assert @env.secrets.to_h.key?(key), "#{key} is missing from .kamal/secrets"
+    end
+  end
+end
